# Giai đoạn 1 checkpoint allows ".trivyignore có lý do" as the alternative to "0 HIGH/CRITICAL".
#
# Unlike the backend services' findings (application libraries, fixable by bumping a pom.xml
# version), every one of these is an OS package (libcrypto3, libssl3, libxml2, musl, zlib, ...)
# inside the `nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:1.27-alpine` base image itself — verified via
# `trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL bss/admin-console:test`, then confirmed NOT a stale local
# cache by `docker pull nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:1.27-alpine` (got a genuinely newer digest)
# + rebuild + rescan: same 34 findings both times. This is upstream's current Alpine package
# snapshot for that tag, not something this Dockerfile controls.
#
# Follow-up options (not done here — each is its own decision, not a quick fix):
#   - Watch for nginxinc to rebuild the 1.27-alpine tag against a newer Alpine base.
#   - Move to a different nginx minor (e.g. 1.29-alpine) once verified compatible.
#   - Consider a minimal/distroless base (e.g. a Chainguard nginx image) — smaller OS attack
#     surface generally, but a bigger switch to evaluate (image size, debugging tools available,
#     compatibility with the existing nginx.conf) than belongs in Giai đoạn 1.

